  speaking most crimes have a statute of  limitations it's in society's interest  for most things to be able to lay to  rest certain offenses that happen  so long ago that it would be impractical  to try and get a conviction years or  decades after the fact  so there's a sunsetting provision after  certain offenses have happened it's not  true for all crimes but it certainly  applies to most of the things that you  can imagine a president having done  before or during office with respect to  obstruction of justice section 32 82 of  Title 18 of the u.s. code is the general  statute of limitations that applies when  there isn't a more specific statute of  limitations it states that except as  otherwise expressly provided by law a  prosecution for a non-capital offense  shall be instituted within five years  after the offense was committed so  unless there is a more specific statute  of limitations in the federal system  it's generally a five-year statute so  practically speaking even if a sitting  president committed the crime of  obstruction of justice what happens if  the president is president for more than  five years what if the president wins a  second term and has committed this  particular crime theoretically if a  president committed a crime of  obstruction of justice and then won his  second term then becoming president  could be like a get-out-of-jail-free  card which I don't think anyone would  support and that also raises the  questions about offenses that might have  been committed before taking office  those offenses may not have a 5-year  statute of limitations there could be a  two or four year statute limitations for  fraud related offenses or there could be  a very specific 1-year statute of  limitations as sometimes happens in  statutory law this is an untested part  of the law Robert Muller's position is  perfectly reasonable that you cannot  indict a sitting president at least  criminally speaking and the corollary to  that argument would probably be that  well you can't actually indict the  sitting president with a crime that  means that the statute of limitations  should be tolled for that period of time  but it's entirely possible that if the  question ever got to the Supreme Court  the Supreme Court says no no mr. Muller  was incorrect you can indict a sitting  president and as a result the statute of  limitations is not told and also by the  way you waited too long to bring the  crime and therefore the statute of  limitations has expired in the president  can no longer be charged with this  offense even after leaving the  presidency that's the problem with  having these untested constitutional  questions that only the supreme court  can answer in this context which brings  us to the next open question we don't  know to what degree of certainty mr.  Muller thinks that the President may or  may not have committed the crime of  obstruction of justice and knowing to  what degree of certainty the evidence  rises would probably help the electorate  and members of Congress to know what  they should be doing in terms of next  steps while the Muller report itself is  often silent about how strong Muller  thinks the evidence is attorney general  Barr in his press conference shortly  before the redacted Muller report was  released said that Muller told him in  person that he wouldn't have found a  crime even in the absence of the legal  limitations that were created as a  result of the framework under which  Muller operated on March 5th we  specifically asked him about the OLC  opinion and whether or not he was taking  the position that he would have found a  crime but for the existence of the OLC  opinion and he made it very clear  several times that that was not his  position frankly given the descriptions  of the factual evidence regarding  obstruction it's hard to believe that  Muller actually said he would not have  made an actual determination in fact the  MOL report itself says that it goes to  great pains to avoid the traditional  prosecutorial decisions and in fact the  molar report says that while he did find  evidence of conspiracy to collude with  Russians that evidence did not rise to  the level of a chargeable offense  so if Muller was capable and willing to  find that the evidence did not rise to  the level of a chargeable conspiracy  with respect to volume one of the Moller  report it doesn't make any sense that he  would have in secret felt the same way  about volume to the obstruction offenses  but left it out of the Muller report  itself and only told the attorney  General William bar that frankly does  not sound accurate which leads me to the  next open question about Attorney  General Bar in bars for page summary of  the Mullen report Attorney General bar  states that he along with Deputy  Attorney General Rod Rosenstein looked  at the evidence that Robert Muller  created regarding obstruction of justice  and came to a determination that there  was no obstruction we don't know how bar  and Rosenstein came to this  determination and we also don't know if  it was based on bars view of the  evidence and facts or whether it was  specifically based on bars view of the  president's article to power as we  covered in a prior video bar has a  fairly idiosyncratic view that if the  president is exercising his article to  power it cannot possibly give rise to  the crime of obstruction of justice  the exercise of that power is  effectively unchecked by the judiciary  there are some scholars that agree with  that view of power but most people  disagree so it's important to know how  bar and Rosen Stein came to the  conclusion that there was no obstruction  of justice we also don't know what is  behind the redactions of the redacted  Muller report by one count there were  eight hundred and fifty five separate  redactions on the basis of things like  harm to ongoing matters and grand jury  testimony that is protected by rule 6 e  on the one hand this is not the most  redacted document I have ever seen in my  legal career I've seen documents that  are far more blacked out than this and  it's hard to argue that redacting  information on the basis that it's going  to harm an ongoing criminal  investigation is a bad thing to do but  on the other hand given the disparity  between the bar for page summary and the  actual redacted Muller report I'm not  sure that this Justice Department  deserves the benefit of the doubt with  respect to the redactions that were made  so it is an open question as to what is  underneath all of these redactions and  what we will see in the weeks and months  going forward as is tested in the courts  whether this information  be unredacted and released to the public  or to Congress additionally in one of  the appendices to the redacted Muller  report there are 12 referrals that  started with the Muller investigation  that were made to other US Attorneys  offices throughout the country many of  those referrals are completely unknown  it is blacked out what the referral was  what the subject matter was and we have  no idea what is going to come of these  investigations that started with the  Muller report but were farmed out to the  other US Attorneys offices throughout  the country that have subject matter  jurisdiction over the rest of these  criminal investigations now in terms of  some of the questionable decisions that  the Muller team made at least from a  legal perspective we've already talked  about the legal framework that Muller  created that may or may not be  constitutionally mandated but one of the  things that really sticks out as an  attorney is Muller's decision not to  interview the president Muller stated  that he viewed his role in this  investigation as a fact-finder not to  come to a conclusion about whether a  sitting president has committed a crime  or not but to be able to gather the  relevant evidence while it was still  fresh while the witnesses were still  available to be able to 