 down in terms of lying under oath  or committing perjury when you're simply  saying that you don't have a specific  recollection of the thing that is asked  of you the amount of I don't knows and I  don't recalls is interesting also for  the fact that the president has claimed  to have one of the greatest memories in  the entire world one of the great  memories of all time but that's the  issue with getting written answers they  aren't written by the answer they are  written by the lawyers and simply signed  onto by the person who was asked the  questions but Mr Muller says that he  found Trump's written answers to be  quote inadequate and says that the  special counsel had the power to  subpoena the president to make him sit  for a deposition but Muller refrained  from doing so because issuing a subpoena  would impose quote substantial delay and  he felt like he already had enough  information those are not unreasonable  concerns but given the circumstances  this would have been a very difficult  decision  only time will tell whether it was the  right one so in the wake of the Muller  report let's talk about next steps  Muller recognizes that impeachment is  the preferred and constitutionally  sanctioned mechanism here he  specifically states that Congress can in  fact police wrongdoing by the executive  whether the allegations of the mole  report rise to the level of impeachment  is a political question that I will  leave to you but Muller and his team  concluded that Congress does in fact  have the authority to police obstruction  of justice stating we concluded that  Congress has the authority to prohibit a  president's corrupt use of his authority  in order to protect the integrity of the  administration of justice I think it's  clear that the reason that this is in  the MOL report is that this is a rebuke  of attorney general bars view that you  can't use the president's inherent  authority as a predicate for obstruction  of justice Muller and his team did not  buy that argument moreover Muller  recognizes that actual criminal  prosecution is an option after the  president leaves office as does the OLC  memo on which the DOJ policy not to  indict the sitting president is based  specifically that quote the President  does not have immunity after he leaves  office this is a further rebuke of bars  view about the constitutional unitary  executive defense that would otherwise  have prevented a sitting president from  facing charges related to actions that  he or she took while in office House  judiciary chair Jerry Nadler has issued  a subpoena for the full Moeller report  and the underlying evidence related to  the investigation it remains to be seen  what could be contained in those  redacted portions and of course we've  talked about the referrals that are out  there are dozens of cases that were  referred out and we don't even know the  first thing about the nature of those  cases there are also rumblings that a  few Democratic members of the House are  pushing for impeachment hearings whether  this is a good political move or whether  impeachment even has a chance of passing  is beyond the scope of this channel  that's a political question that I will  leave to you but here's what we can know  for sure about the next steps number one  the courts will decide whether to  release the unredacted Muller report and  the  lying evidence number two the dozen or  so referrals to the other US Attorneys  offices will likely result in further  indictments of whom it's anyone's guess  but recall that one of those referrals  led to the president's personal  Attorney's Office being searched and  that's a huge deal it's likely that at  least some other people will be indicted  and number three elected representatives  will have to decide whether to move  forward with impeachment impeachment is  a political tool and it will be up to  the politicians to decide whether to use  that measure or not one other potential  next step is perhaps